section.section_3_7
    h2 Koba the Marxist
    p
        |A more mature
        span(data-type="person" data-id="JoSt") Koba
        |eventually broke through what he found to be the confining limits of nationality to identify with an explicitly anti-nationalist political party and its socialist future. In rapid succession,
        span(data-type="person" data-id="JoSt") Soso
        |left the largely Georgian town of his birth and entered other cultural milieus: the
        span(data-type="organization" data-id="OrCs") Orthodox Christian seminary
        |, the intelligentsia, the movement, and the party.
    p By his twenty-first birthday, he had become a professional revolutionary defined by a new culture: the political culture of Russian Social Democracy, with its specific forms of moral and personal behavior, its idealized self-representations of what constituted an intelligent (a radical political intellectual), and its elaborate codes of loyalty and sacrifice.
    p
        |During the
        span(data-type="event" data-id="CldW") Cold War
        |, battalions of Soviet and Western scholars explored the history of Russian Social Democracy, the Marxist movement that had split irrevocably into
        span(data-type="organization" data-id="Blsh") Bolshevik
        |and
        span(data-type="organization" data-id="Mesh") Menshevik
        |tendencies by
        span(data-type="date") 1905
        |, and whose radical wing led by
        span(data-type="person" data-id="VlLe") Vladimir Lenin
        |came to power in
        span(data-type="date") October 1917
        |. The stories they told were diametrically opposed to one another, each a product of the intellectual and political imperatives of their respective worlds.
    p
        |With the fall of the
        span(data-type="place" data-id="USSR") Soviet Union
        |, interest in the intricacies of the political struggles of the Marxist factions, and their meanings and influences on the Soviet future evaporated. Yet at the same moment, the less contentious environment of the post-Soviet period, along with the opening of Soviet archives, made possible a reevaluation of the history of Russian (and Georgian) Marxism, and their respective and distinct labor movements.
    p
        |Since those movements and the Marxist underground were the major breeding grounds for
        span(data-type="person" data-id="JoSt") Stalin
        |and those around him, I have undertaken in Stalin: Passage to Revolution a fresh reading of the history of those movements out of which the Soviet experiment and
        span(data-type="person" data-id="JoSt") Stalin
        |himself emerged.